This is the same operational model as Claude Code.<p><pre><code>  ls ~&#x2F;.local&#x2F;share&#x2F;claude&#x2F;versions&#x2F;
  ps auxww | grep &quot;claude bg&quot;
</code></pre>
I see a few prior versions of the Claude Code binary and several running background tasks, without any copy of Claude Code in the foreground. I regularly get a &quot;Update available&quot; message in my Claude Code UI too.

&gt;Test your browser:<p>&gt;coveryourtracks.eff.org shows you how unique your fingerprint is, with entropy for each measured data point.<p>Something to note - if a fingerprint is generic enough, that might be a _good_ thing!<p>Having worked with fingerprinting, anti-fingerprint browsers are very hard to properly identify. The ways to get around it are things like collecting more coarse fingerprints[0], as the overall one is fairly easy to change (e.g. make your browser lie about a collection of browser apis).<p>What&#x27;s imo more difficult though is a fingerprint like:<p>- iPhone &lt;new_version&gt;
- running os &lt;latest_stable&gt;
- using default safari
- bought and used in &lt;common_location&gt; (e.g. usa)<p>on a 5g mast in a decently sized city, you&#x27;ll share a fingerprint with at least one other active user using a setup like ^<p>grapheneOS on the other hand stands out a bit more!<p>[0] - <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;blog.castle.io&#x2F;fingerprints-beyond-device-ids-engineered-representations-for-fraud-detection&#x2F;" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;blog.castle.io&#x2F;fingerprints-beyond-device-ids-engine...</a>
